  "answer_text": "Absolutely \u2014 what we eat matters enormously! Here's a comprehensive overview of why:\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf4e Food Is Central to Health\n\nThe food we eat gives our bodies the \"information\" and materials they need to function properly. If we don't get the right information, our metabolic processes suffer and our health declines. If we get too much food, or food that gives our bodies the wrong instructions, we can become overweight, undernourished, and at risk for the development of diseases and conditions, such as arthritis, diabetes, and heart disease.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83d\udd2c It's Not Just About Calories\n\nWhen it comes to staying healthy, it may not be enough to just count calories \u2014 what you choose to eat really does matter. As one nutrition expert put it, \"you can't judge a food by its calorie count.\"\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf3f Diet and Disease Risk\n\nDiet plays a critical role in shaping the health and well-being of both individuals and populations, and unhealthy diets are a major risk factor for disease and disability.\n\nFor decades, it has been known that the occurrence and course of a variety of typical Western diseases such as cancer, stroke, and myocardial infarction are affected by diet. Specifically, research shows that a diet rich in red meat and processed meat leads to a high risk of cancer, especially colorectal cancer. Substantial evidence indicates that eating more vegetables and fruits and less meat is able to decrease overall cancer risk and improve health.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83e\udde0 Diet and Mental Health\n\nEating a poor diet reduces physical and mental health because eating healthy allows people to be more active. Two-thirds of people who eat fresh fruit and vegetables daily report no mental health issues, as reported by the Mental Health Foundation. Those who do have some level of mental health problems have generally reported eating less healthy foods and more unhealthy foods.\n\n---\n\n### \u2696\ufe0f Diet and Weight\n\nEating a nutritious diet filled with fruits, vegetables, whole grains, dairy, and protein will help maintain a healthy body weight. Studies evaluating the effects of an ultra-processed diet compared to a minimally processed diet find that an overall healthy diet containing plenty of fruits, vegetables, and whole grains can help prevent weight gain.\n\n---\n\n### \ud83c\udf0d The Bigger Picture\n\nDiet is not just about giving your body sustenance \u2014 nourishing yourself engages every aspect of your being: physical, social, emotional, mental, and spiritual. The exact make-up of a diversified, balanced, and healthy diet will vary depending on individual characteristics such as age, gender, lifestyle, and degree of physical activity, as well as cultural context, locally available foods, and dietary customs.\n\n---\n\nIn summary, **yes \u2014 what we eat matters greatly**, both in the short and long term. A balanced, varied, and minimally processed diet is consistently linked to better physical health, mental well-being, and a longer life.",
